WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.280
In Avengers: Endgame, the dusted heroes arrive
just in time to assist the surviving Avengers

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.620
in taking down Thanos.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:10.300
Just as Captain America thought he'd have
to go it alone, portals begin opening across

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.160
the sky.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.360
The very first hero to step through is Black
Panther, and we finally know why.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.140
Avengers: Endgame co-director Joe Russo recently
revealed during a Reddit Ask Me Anything session

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.580
that the King of Wakanda wasn't technically
the top choice to return first, Sam Wilson,

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:26.060
a.k.a. Falcon, was.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:30.240
Joe and his filmmaking brother Anthony Russo
wanted Sam to be the first person to communicate

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.880
with Captain America during the battle with
Thanos.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.400
"Cap, it's Sam.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.520
Can you hear me?

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:40.900
On your left."

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.060
Since Sam was fighting in Wakanda when he
disintegrated in Avengers: Infinity War, the

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.620
directors knew that they needed to have the
Wakandan portal be the first one to open up

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.860
after Professor Hulk's Snap.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.340
Given that Sam isn't the most important person
in Wakanda, T'Challa wound up being the first

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:57.380
hero to emerge.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.860
In Endgame, Sam is the hero who is heard first,
telling Cap through his communications device

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.500
that the heroes are arriving to his left,
but Black Panther is the first one seen.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.680
Russo added that:

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.479
"We spent a lot of time in the edit room playing
around with the sequencing of the portals.

00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:14.029
We probably didn't lock that section of the
movie until about a month before the film

00:01:14.029 --> 00:01:14.880
was in theaters.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.820
We always wanted Sam to be the first one to
communicate with Cap via his comm and Sam

00:01:18.829 --> 00:01:23.170
was last in Wakanda, so logically the first
portal that would open would be the Wakandan

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.859
portal...the first person that would logically
walk through a portal from Wakanda would be

00:01:26.859 --> 00:01:30.899
the king himself, bringing his army once again
to the defense of Cap and the world."

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:35.169
Joe added on the audio commentary track included
on the digital release of Avengers: Endgame:

00:01:35.169 --> 00:01:38.899
"Who else would you want to have your back
in this moment other than T'Challa and Wakanda?"

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:42.490
Sam letting Cap know that he's got someone
coming in on his left is also a reference

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:46.639
to a fan-favorite moment in Captain America:
The Winter Soldier, in which the Star-Spangled

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.740
Avenger keeps lapping Sam on their run.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.020
"Don't you say it, don't you say it."

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:54.520
"On your left."

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.440
"Come on!"

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.720
In the end, Black Panther being chosen as
the first hero to return after Professor Hulk's

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.100
Snap wasn't down to some big, emotional reason.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.560
It wasn't even due to T'Challa and Wakanda
being some of the Avengers' most loyal allies.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.400
The real explanation has a lot more to do
with logistics.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.680
They wanted Sam to communicate with Cap first,
Sam was in Wakanda when he was snapped away

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.680
into the ether, and Black Panther is the king
of the nation, so he showed up first.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:18.720
Easy breezy.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.580
The issue of which hero would return first
after the Snap in Avengers: Endgame wasn't

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.140
the only logistical issue the film's team
had to tackle.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.300
Endgame co-writer Stephen McFeely revealed
on the Endgame commentary track that he and

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.989
his writing partner Christopher Markus wrote
a version of the script in which the heroes

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:34.860
came back and were shown immediately:

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.040
"Just on an act-construction level, we were
faced with a strange problem where we needed

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.849
two snaps of the Gauntlet and asked ourselves
often, 'What does it mean to bring everybody

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:46.969
back?'...We certainly tried a version where
everyone came back and you knew it immediately,

00:02:46.969 --> 00:02:50.480
and there they were...Doing that prevented
us from a really heroic reveal."

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.680
And what a heroic reveal it wound up being.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.960
Black Panther entering alongside Shuri and
Okoye, then giving Captain America a knowing

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.480
nod as Sam flies overhead?

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.340
We're getting chills just thinking about it.

